ing like her life  journey what she's had to overcome to  become the woman that  she is and like just knowing that where  you come from doesn't define who you can  be tomorrow now you've had such like an  inspirational story like those of people  who don't know you would like born in a  refugee camp and then you came to the US  when you were seven yeah what was your  like first memory of that refugee camp  first of all I remember running around  almost butt-naked at times but let's be  honest like that was the best part about  growing up in a refugee camp is you got  your hands dirty you got your feet dirty  and it was like go out and play until  like the Sun Goes Down and then run back  run back home but it was definitely  challenging in terms of like not knowing  what your next meal is coming and like  malaria and like just the element and  like the fact that your entire future  your entire life livelihood is in limbo  at all times like that was devastating  and I think as like children like at six  I couldn't tell you what a refugee meant  or why I was that way because all I  remember was the camp I was born and  raised there it was home it was all I've  ever known so I had a great childhood  because I didn't know any better but it  was definitely a happy childhood in  terms of community I mean the refugee  camp it was like the largest I think it  still is the largest in Kenya you know  Kakuma is one of the biggest camps in  the world so it was refugees from all  over Africa and a lot of them were my  friends and so getting to alike learn  Swahili and speaking fluent Somali and  running around and singing like my  childhood was comprised of songs and  that was beautiful that's something that  I would never ever like change for the  world I wouldn't have had my life any  other way how do you think they seven  years of shape to you as a person it  made me rich in terms of I know what  brings me joy true joy is having friends  like having friendships even at six  seven like cultivating those  relationships it would have been so  boring if my camp was made out of just  other Somali boys and girls like I  couldn't tell you how like bland that  would have been probably right it's made  me rich  in terms of knowing what brings joy to  me to my heart you know and knowing that  you'll survive like I was fine in the  camp like we were thriving in other ways  right  so that that a resiliency of knowing  that no matter what life what curveball  life throws at me I know I'll be able to  tackle it I'm gonna be able to survive  it I'll know I'll get through it because  I've gotten through worse but I think  it's so amazing you can sit here and say  something that so many people who sees a  negative experience to say it's like  such an empowering experience well when  you have nothing but that you are forced  to see the beauty in the littlest things  the simplest things you are appreciative  you are grateful there is no such thing  as like a horrible horrible childhood  and then in a camp like I think there's  always a good side there is always a  bright side to any situation now you're  going to achieve all these incredible  things like being a pastor for UNICEF  like being the face of these incredible  brands do you feel like you're doing it  for that girl who was in that refugee  camp I see myself and I always think  like what would what would  twelve-year-old me what what message  could I have used you know that's why I  always tell girls don't change yourself  change the game and when I when I say  that I really mean be confident who you  are like where your identity with pride  no matter what that looks like no matter  what that is  we're probably and the right people will  accept you for you and you don't need to  fit the mold you don't need to fit the  mold you are just fine the way that you  are there were my campaigns and through  fashion and through this platform I keep  thinking like oh maybe I'm like telling  my younger self a message that I wish I  would have heard throughout your whole  life how is your relationship with your  hijab changed when I was younger I  definitely wasn't adventurous when it  comes to my hijab it was more about okay  I'm gonna just wrap my hair up and like  I didn't know much about fashion and  honestly I treated to clothes like I  just need something to cover my back  when I'm leaving the house fashion has  taught me so much in terms of how to  make my hijab customized to my  personality like this is an edgier look  this is younger I've gotten to  experiment soul in so many ways  well the last three years compared to  any any other time in my life  and it's just been incredible I just  want girls to know like just because you  wear the hijab doesn't mean like you're  not a young girl yourself like you still  should be able to experiment and make it  customized to your personality which is  such a great message to send out there  because so many people say so many  negative things and like actually you're  actually there saying this is actually a  symbol of my own voice it's what I grew  up seeing you know like my mom wore it  and then like I always looked up to her  and it's like lipstick if your mom wears  lipstick you kind of want to emulate her  beauty you kind of want to like if your  mom is walking around in heels like so  many little girls are like in boys I did  it there you go and I think thing for me  with the scarf I was like I wanted I  want to look like you I want to look  like her I'm definitely experimenting  when it comes to like ways to tie my  scarf ways to achieve turban looks  because girls are on my Instagram  telling me oh yes this this is good and  oh you really tried it and like oh you  know I mean like I love the feedback  that I get from other girls who wear the  hijab who maybe aren't in fashion and  maybe will never like get to experience  like working with a stylist but then  they can go to my Instagram page and  they can like make 